  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal system nearby will maintain rain\nchances today with below average temperatures.\n\nA mid-level disturbance will pivot from the eastern Ohio Valley\ntoward the mid-Atlantic region today. While there are broad\npatches of light to moderate drizzle extending from the central\nShenandoah Valley to Northeast Maryland, a light steadier rain\nis developing in southwestern Virginia just ahead of the mid-\nlevel disturbance. Much of this light rain should be along and\neast of I-81 and remain mostly along and south of I-66 through\nmidday today. A persistent northeast wind should keep\ntemperatures on the cool side widespread today into tonight.\nHighs will be mainly in the 60s. Any rain chances should come to\nan end around Sunday evening. Overnight conditions should be \nsimilar to last night with lows again in the 50s.\n\nAs a deep upper trough tracks across far eastern Canada, a more\npotent cold front sweeps through the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday. This will favor increasing sunshine, but also a large\nuptick in northwesterly winds. Afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25\nmph are possible, but with highs returning to the upper 60s to\nmid 70s (upper 50s to 60s in the mountains).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freezing temperatures are possible to\nthe west of the Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nA strong area of high pressure will build over the area next \nweek, leading to an extended period of dry conditions. As a cooler \nairmass works in and high pressure builds overhead, frost and \nfreezing temperatures may be possible to the west of the Blue Ridge \nboth Monday night and Tuesday night. We'll start to see slightly \nwarmer air build in from the south and west later in the week. High \ntemperatures at lower elevations are forecast to be in the 60s \n(upper 50s/low 60s mountains) on Tuesday, upper 60s to low 70s on \nWednesday (60s mountains), and mid 70s (mid-upper 60s mountains) on \nWednesday and Thursday. Low temperatures will gradually moderate \nfrom the 30s/40s early in the week, to the upper 40s/low 50s later \nin the week.",
